{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon CloudFront announces cross-account support for VPC origins - AWS","context":"Amazon CloudFront has introduced cross-account support for Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) origins, enabling customers to access VPC origins in different AWS accounts from their CloudFront distributions. Previously, customers could only access VPC origins in the same AWS account as CloudFront. This limitation forced customers to keep their origins in public subnets, which increased security risks and operational complexity. With cross-account VPC origins, customers can use AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM) to grant CloudFront access to origins in private VPCs in different accounts, both within and outside their AWS Organizations and organizational units (OUs). This feature is available in AWS Commercial Regions and comes at no additional cost. By using cross-account VPC origins, customers can maintain their multi-account architecture for better security isolation, cost management, and compliance while enjoying the benefits of VPC origins' inherent security. VPC origins can be used with Application Load Balancers (ALB), Network Load Balancers (NLB), and EC2 Instances in private subnets. This feature simplifies security management and reduces operational complexity, making it easy to use CloudFront as the single front door for applications","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/amazon-cloudfront-cross-account-vpc-origins/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"cloudfront","skill_description":"Configures Amazon CloudFront content delivery across six workflows: when to use CloudFront and how it fits with AWS WAF, Shield, CloudFront Functions, Lambda@Edge, Route 53, and origins (creating a distribution, caching, and Flat Rate Pricing (FRP) versus pay-as-you-go pricing); managing custom-domain TLS certificates (ACM in us-east-1); configuring multi-tenant distributions; protecting origins with origin access control (OAC), VPC origins, and origin mutual TLS (mTLS); securing content with signed URLs and cookies, geographic restrictions, viewer mutual TLS, and edge token validation; and observing traffic with standard and real-time logs. Applicable when the customer wants to put CloudFront in front of content, choose pricing, lock an origin, restrict who can view content, or analyze logs. Not applicable for the Route 53 DNS side of a CloudFront custom domain or failover between distributions, or for pure-Route 53 DNS work (see the route53 skill).\n\n\nServices: cloudfront, s3, acm, vpc\nTasks: configure, secure, deploy\nPersona: developer, devops, sre\nWorkload: networking, content-delivery, security","skill_name":"cloudfront"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon CloudFront announces VPC origins - AWS","context":"Amazon CloudFront has introduced a new feature called VPC origins, which enables customers to use CloudFront to deliver content from applications hosted in private subnets of their Virtual Private Cloud (VPC). This feature allows the use of Application Load Balancers (ALB), Network Load Balancers (NLB), and EC2 Instances in a private subnet, enhancing security and maintaining high-performance and global scalability","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2024/11/amazon-cloudfront-vpc-origins/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"Amazon CloudFront announces mutual TLS support for origins - AWS","context":"Amazon CloudFront has introduced mutual TLS (mTLS) support for origins, allowing customers to authenticate requests from their CloudFront distributions to origin servers using TLS certificates. This eliminates the need for custom security controls and reduces operational overhead. Origin mTLS is supported for origins on AWS, such as Application Load Balancer and API Gateway, as well as on-premises and custom origins. Customers can use certificates issued by AWS Private Certificate Authority or third-party private Certificate Authorities, which they import through AWS Certificate Manager. Origin mTLS is available at no extra cost and is supported in the Business and Premium pricing plans. Customers can configure origin mTLS using various methods, including the AWS Management Console, CLI, SDK, CDK, or CloudFormation. Origin mTLS provides cryptographic verification of CloudFront's identity and ensures that only verified CloudFront distributions can connect to backend infrastructure","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/01/amazon-cloudfront-mutual-tls-for-origins/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"Amazon CloudFront now supports TLS 1.3 for origin connections - AWS","context":"Amazon CloudFront has implemented support for Transport Layer Security (TLS) 1.3 when connecting to origins, enhancing security and improving performance for origin communications. This upgrade offers stronger encryption algorithms, reduced handshake latency, and a better overall security posture for data transmission between CloudFront edge locations and origin servers. TLS 1.3 is automatically enabled for all origin types, including custom origins, Amazon S3, and Application Load Balancers, with no configuration changes required. TLS 1.3 provides faster connection establishment through a reduced number of round trips during the handshake process, delivering up to 30% improvement in connection performance when the origin supports it. CloudFront will automatically negotiate TLS 1.3 when the origin supports it, while maintaining backward compatibility with lower TLS versions for origins that haven't yet upgraded. This enhancement benefits applications requiring high security standards, such as financial services, healthcare, and e-commerce platforms that handle sensitive data. TLS 1.3 support for origin connections is available at no additional charge in all CloudFront edge locations. For more information about CloudFront origin TLS, refer to the Amazon CloudFront Developer Guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/amazon-cloudfront-tls13-origin/"}]}}"}]}], 